  "query_preview": "..\n\nA weak frontal boundary marked by a corridor of dew points in the \nupper 60s and 70s remains draped NE-SW across Lower MI into northern \nIN this morning. This will slowly migrate eastward today while \nwhat's left of any frontal structure continues to fade. By tonight \nand into Friday, the front dissipates overhead leaving the area \nwithin weak southwest flow downstream of a longwave trough over the \nMidwest. Additional moisture will work into the area from the Ohio \nand Mississippi Valleys with weak perturbations aloft bringing bouts \nof scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms today and Friday. \nGiven the weak/nebulous forcing, any precip is expected to be hit-or-\nmiss and timing generally carries lower than normal confidence \nthrough this period.\n\nGuidance favors mainly dry conditions much of the morning before \ndaytime heating produces a weakly unstable and uncapped environment \nsupportive of scattered pop-up showers and isolated thunderstorms \nthrough the afternoon. Upper jet entrance region forcing arrives \nsouth of M-59 during this window, suggesting the highest coverage of \nshowers sets up there (~40%). HREF/REFS ensemble mean SBCAPE on the \norder of 500 to 1000 J/kg and mean 0-6 km shear of 25 to 30 kt \nsupports potential for a few stronger updrafts capable of producing \nheavy downpours and isolated gusty winds up to 40 mph. Coverage \nshould wane this evening with the loss of diurnal instability, but \nanother flare-up of convection will be possible in the south \novernight on the nose of a weak LLJ. Otherwise, it will be another \nmild and muggy night with potential for some patchy fog by early \nFriday.\n\nA similar weakly forced and uncapped environment exists on Friday, \nwith a slight uptick in instability outlined in ensemble \nprobabilities. The favored window for showers and storms will be the \nmid afternoon to mid evening diurnal peak, with CAMs suggestive of \nenough surface convergence to trigger convection. Severe weather \nchances are low given weak lapse rates, shear, and organization.\n\nA Pacific jet streak tracking into the upper Midwest Friday night \nwill force a cold front across the Great Lakes on Saturday. The more \ncoherent upper forcing tied to the nose of the jet and associated \nshortwave may outpace the front itself Saturday morning, but enough \nfrontal convergence and instability exists for a chance of showers \nand storms during the day. Temperature advection is minimal with \nthis front, and seasonable August temperatures are forecast to \npersist this weekend and into next week. Post-frontal high pressure \non Sunday looks to bring some reprieve from the unsettled pattern.\n\nWill need to monitor Sunday night into Monday which presents the \nnext window for more organized convection as several stronger \nshortwaves pass over a warm/stationary front in the vicinity. The \nlast several runs of ensemble guidance has hinted at potential for \nheavier rainfall, with latest NBM QMD offering 50% probability for \n1+\" by 12z Tuesday. LREF is more muted but still shows a wide swath \nof 25% probability for 1+\" in the region. This passes the eye test \ngiven the frontal setup, LLJ forcing, and moisture rich environment.",
